      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "The aim of the survey was to elucidate the significance of postoperative pain therapy for the patient and its influence on the choice of hospital."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "This prospective, anonymous survey of consecutive patients in a general surgical clinic was performed by an independent study nurse."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "A total of 161 patients were included; 90% of the surgical patients considered \"good pain therapy\" as a highly important factor, and three of four patients would admit themselves more likely to a clinic well known for \"good pain therapy.\" If the patients could choose their hospital, factors such as \"medical care by the physicians\" are most important (rank 1; rank 1 most important, rank 10 most unimportant) followed by quality of \"nursing care\" (mean rank 2.6) and \"good pain therapy\" (mean rank 3.6). Older patients (> or = 60 years) preferred a hospital with known \"good pain therapy\" more often for surgical therapy than younger patients (< 60 years)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "It can be concluded that postoperative pain relief is an important factor for the patients' selection of a clinic and is influenced by an age of > or = 60 years."
        }
      ]},
